---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import InputDemo from "../../../components/InputDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";

const usageExample = `import { Field } from "@/components/ui/field";
import { Input } from "@/components/ui/input";

<Field label="Email" error={errors.email} required>
  {(control) => <Input type="email" autoComplete="email" {...control} />}
</Field>`;
---

<ComponentPage slug="input">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    It is a plain <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, so every native type, attribute and browser feature,
    such as autofill, keeps working.
  </Fragment>

  <InputDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Usage</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      An input always needs a visible label. Put it in a Field, which renders the label and wires
      the description, the error and <code>aria-invalid</code> for you:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={usageExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>Visible border and focus.</strong> The border uses the <code>input</code> token,
        which has at least 3:1 contrast with the background. Keyboard focus shows the ring outline,
        which also stays visible in forced-colors mode.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Invalid.</strong> With <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> the border turns
        destructive. The color only reinforces the error: the Field's error text and icon say what
        is wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Disabled.</strong> A <code>disabled</code> input is dimmed, shows a not-allowed
        cursor and is skipped by the keyboard. Prefer <code>readOnly</code> for a value people still
        need to read or copy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Size.</strong> It is at least 40px tall, like the default Button, and uses a 16px
        font, so mobile browsers do not zoom in when it takes focus.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/input.tsx</code>, the same path as shadcn's Input, and needs only
    your <code>cn()</code> helper. If your <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn
    version, the CLI asks before replacing it; answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
